# G021 — Text Editor

*v2. See `text-editor.md` for prose: first stateful object in the
Rosetta Stone, undo stack as event sourcing, buffer as document,
cursor as attention primitive, find/replace as content rewriting
engine.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Collaborative Editing Session

[collaborative-edit @document-id
    IN ORDER TO
        load a vault document as a text-editor buffer
        accept edits from multiple agents each with their own cursor
        and support undo as a reverse walk of the event stream

    @buffer <- [editor[load {path: @document-id}]]

    sync @edits {
        insert @agent @pos @text ->
            [editor[insert {buffer: @buffer, pos: @pos, text: @text}]]
            [editor[push-event {buffer: @buffer,
                                 op: {kind: "insert",
                                       agent: @agent,
                                       pos: @pos, text: @text}}]]

        delete @agent @pos @length ->
            @removed <- [editor[delete
                                  {buffer: @buffer,
                                   pos: @pos, length: @length}]]
            [editor[push-event {buffer: @buffer,
                                 op: {kind: "delete",
                                       agent: @agent, pos: @pos,
                                       removed: @removed}}]]

        undo ->
            [editor[undo {buffer: @buffer}]]
            ;; walks event stack backward, inverting each op

        find-replace @needle @replacement ->
            [editor[find-replace-all
                     {buffer: @buffer,
                      needle: @needle,
                      replacement: @replacement}]]
    }

    [editor[save {buffer: @buffer, path: @document-id}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[editor
    new              {initial-text}
    load             {path}
    save             {buffer, path}
    insert           {buffer, pos, text}
    delete           {buffer, pos, length}
    cursor           {buffer}                 ;; -> attention position
    set-cursor       {buffer, pos}
    undo             {buffer}
    redo             {buffer}
    find             {buffer, needle}         ;; -> [position]
    find-replace-all {buffer, needle, replacement}
    push-event       {buffer, op}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: undo as inverse-walk `where` every op has a reverse

[editor[undo {buffer: @b}]
    IN ORDER TO
        restore the prior state by popping and inverting the last event
        so the undo stack IS the document's history, replayable either way

    @last <- pop from @b:undo-stack
    @last:kind
        where "insert"
            -> delete inserted range
        where "delete"
            -> re-insert the removed text at its position
        where "find-replace"
            -> walk replacements in reverse, swap back
    push inverse onto redo-stack
]

Every operation has a reverse. The buffer's current state is the
fold of all events from the beginning — same shape as G100's
version store, same shape as Git.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: cursor as agent attention

The cursor is a single position that receives the agent's focus.
Multiple agents in a collaborative session each carry their own
cursor — the noosphere's notion of who is reading where is nothing
more than a buffer plus a per-agent cursor position.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @b <- [editor[new {initial-text: "Hello world"}]]
    [editor[insert {buffer: @b, pos: 5, text: ","}]]
    -> [editor[text {buffer: @b}]]           ;; -> "Hello, world"

    [editor[undo {buffer: @b}]]
    -> [editor[text {buffer: @b}]]           ;; -> "Hello world"

    [editor[find-replace-all
             {buffer: @b, needle: "world",
              replacement: "InnateScript"}]]
    -> [editor[text {buffer: @b}]]           ;; -> "Hello InnateScript"
]
